    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The order matters and it is the single most common source of bugs here: **row first, then column**. `grid[1][2]` means row 1, column 2 — not the other way round."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A helpful way to keep it straight: the first index picks *which line you are on*, and the second picks *how far along that line you are*. You choose the row before you can walk along it."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Valid ranges"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Each index obeys the same rule as a 1D array — it starts at 0 and stops one short of the count:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Row index runs from `0` to `rows - 1`",
        "Column index runs from `0` to `columns - 1`"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For a 3 x 4 grid, the last valid cell is `grid[2][3]`. Notice that neither index goes up to 3 and 4; writing `grid[3][4]` is out of bounds on both counts."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Square is a special case, not the rule"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Grids are often drawn square in examples, which quietly teaches people that rows and columns are interchangeable. They are not. A 3 x 4 grid and a 4 x 3 grid hold the same number of cells and are completely different shapes. Always read the shape before you write the loops."
    }
  ],
